INT. CIVIL COURTHOUSE - JURY DELIBERATION ROOM - AFTERNOON

A windowless room. A long laminate table, twelve chairs, a water pitcher. A wall clock ticking too loudly.

Four jurors remain after a long lunch. The rest have filed out for a break.

FOREWOMAN BEATRICE KOWALSKI-DAVIES (50s, practical blazer, reading glasses) leafs through her notes. JUROR THADDEUS OKAFOR (30s, hoodie, bitten nails) taps his pen on the table. JUROR PRUDENCE LINDSTROM (60s, floral blouse, quiet) arranges a row of peppermints in front of her. And JUROR MARCELINO ALVARADO (40s, work boots, a thermos of coffee) leans back in his chair, staring at the ceiling.

BEATRICE
Let's recap where we are.

THADDEUS
We're nowhere.

PRUDENCE
(gently)
We're somewhere, dear.

MARCELINO
We're exactly where we started.

Beatrice sets down her notes, removes her glasses, rubs the bridge of her nose. Thaddeus stops tapping. Prudence unwraps a peppermint and offers it across the table. Marcelino takes it, pops it into his mouth, and Thaddeus declines. Beatrice puts her glasses back on and looks at each of them in turn.

BEATRICE
The plaintiff says the contract was breached on March third.

THADDEUS
The defendant says it was never signed.

PRUDENCE
The signature looked genuine.

MARCELINO
It looked traced.

BEATRICE
Mr. Alvarado, you keep saying that.

MARCELINO
I keep seeing it.

THADDEUS
I think she's lying. The plaintiff.

PRUDENCE
She didn't seem like a liar.

THADDEUS
The good ones never do.

BEATRICE
We can't convict on a feeling.

THADDEUS
It's not a criminal case.

BEATRICE
We can't find liability on one either.

Silence. The clock ticks. Prudence folds the peppermint wrapper into a small square. Marcelino drains his thermos. Thaddeus resumes tapping.

The door opens. A court officer leans in.

OFFICER
Madam Foreman, the judge wants to know if you've reached a verdict.

BEATRICE
Not yet.

OFFICER
How much longer?

BEATRICE
Another hour.

OFFICER
I'll tell her.

The door closes. Beatrice sighs, takes a fresh sheet, and writes a single word at the top.

THADDEUS
What did you write?

BEATRICE
"Evidence."

She underlines it twice. Prudence nods. Marcelino leans forward. Thaddeus, last, sets down his pen and slides his chair closer to the table.
